    <briefdescription>
<para>The integrals over the smoothing domain of a particle: its volume and the smoothed gradients of the shape functions (stabilized conforming nodal integration, Chen et al., 2001). </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The smoothed gradient is the average of the gradient over the smoothing domain <formula id="0">$ \Omega_s $</formula>, turned into a boundary integral by the divergence theorem and integrated with one point per face <formula id="1">$ f $</formula>, the face center <formula id="2">$ \boldsymbol{Y}_f $</formula> (<ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_particle_section_integrals_1aa6934f8efb3f0ccf494e4eb9dda18daa" kindref="member">faceQuadraturePoints()</ref>),     <formula id="3">\[  \tilde N_{A,i} = \frac{1}{|\Omega_s|}\int_{\Omega_s} \frac{\partial N_A}{\partial Y_i}\,dV
  = \frac{1}{|\Omega_s|}\oint_{\partial\Omega_s} N_A\,n_i\,dA
  \approx \frac{1}{|\Omega_s|} \sum_f N_A(\boldsymbol{Y}_f)\, n_{f,i}\, dA_f ,
\]</formula> with the coordinates <formula id="4">$ \boldsymbol{Y} $</formula> of the intermediate configuration, the volume <formula id="5">$ |\Omega_s| $</formula> (<ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_particle_section_integrals_1a2388a9797c28a71da7edbfa6f228fd51" kindref="member">volume()</ref>) and the area vector <formula id="6">$ \boldsymbol{n}_f\,dA_f $</formula> of face <formula id="1">$ f $</formula> (<ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_lagrange_cell_1a4bc603c3140a1fd53d602a1e934f912e" kindref="member">LagrangeCell::boundarySurfaceVector()</ref>). The face rule is the only approximation, and only the values of the shape functions at the face points are needed. <ref refid="namespace_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_n_s_n_i" kindref="compound">NSNI</ref> smooths the second derivatives alike, from the (unsmoothed) gradients at the same points,    <formula id="7">\[  \hat N_{A,IJ} = \frac{1}{|\Omega_s|} \sum_f \frac{\partial N_A}{\partial Y_I}(\boldsymbol{Y}_f)\,
  n_{f,J}\, dA_f ,
\]</formula> stored unsymmetrized as <computeroutput>d2N_dYdY[J](I, A)</computeroutput>; <ref refid="namespace_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_n_s_n_i_1ae076b82f54fbecb8d1a85e6a1b9ede7e" kindref="member">NSNI::secondGradientOfNode()</ref> symmetrizes them.</para>
<para>The particles take these integrals, and their evaluation points (<ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_particle_section_integrals_1aa6934f8efb3f0ccf494e4eb9dda18daa" kindref="member">faceQuadraturePoints()</ref>), from here and not from the cell: the face rule of the smoothing is defined in this class only. <emphasis>Section</emphasis> is meant as for finite elements (plane strain, 3D): the form of the integrals depends on it. Here the section is Cartesian, and the integrals carry the weight 1. The class holds static functions only, so that a particle selects its integrals with its own template parameters.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>nDim</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The spatial dimension. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>nVertices</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The number of vertices of the cell (4: Quad, 8: Hexa). </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
